Q: Google signin for aws cognito using Amplify not working. | signInWithRedirect in amplify not working in React native A: <p>This an old issue but knowing how painful it is to solve Amplify related issues, I'm sharing what worked for me.</p>
<p>What worked for me was implementing a custom authSessionOpener to use expo-web-browser</p>
<pre><code>import { signInWithRedirect } from 'aws-amplify/auth';
import * as WebBrowser from 'expo-web-browser';

await signInWithRedirect({
  provider: 'Google',
  options: {
    authSessionOpener: async (url, redirectUrls, prefersEphemeralSession) =&gt; {
      const redirectUrl = redirectUrls[0];
      const result = await WebBrowser.openAuthSessionAsync(url, redirectUrl, {
        preferEphemeralSession: prefersEphemeralSession,
      });

      if (result.type === 'success' &amp;&amp; result.url) {
        return { type: 'success', url: result.url };
      }
      if (result.type === 'cancel' || result.type === 'dismiss') {
        return { type: 'canceled' };
      }
      return { type: 'error', error: new Error('OAuth session failed') };
    },
  },
});
</code></pre>

Q: Sort JSON in alphabetical order by its key in Java A: <p>When using Jackson, you can order an object created from a map like:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>import com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonPropertyOrder;

public class SomeResponse {

    @JsonPropertyOrder(alphabetic = true) // add annotation on Map field
    private Map&lt;String, Object&gt; data;     // HashMap or similar

}
</code></pre>
<p>There is no need to apply any ordering on the map itself: Jackson can do this on serializing to JSON.</p>

Q: xml2js - always return an array for certain nodes A: <p>I came searching for an answer to the same question, I thought maybe there was a magic extra xml2js option I didn't know about. It seems not! It's an array, but if only one element, it's an object. I dealt with it like this...</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const things = Array.isArray(possibleArray) ? possibleArray : [possibleArray];

for(const thing of things){
    ...
}
</code></pre>
